{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend (e.g., Example 3/4/5) but sentiment remains near zero or low, the model systematically underestimates volatility and fails to capture sudden mean-reversion spikes; apply an upward bias correction of +10% to +20% on the first 5 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "When history shows a consolidation range with low sentiment (e.g., Example 1/2), the model tends to be overly smooth and misses sharp upward inflection points; increase the slope of the forecast curve by 15-25% during the initial 7-day window.",
    "The model exhibits significant lag in reacting to sentiment spikes (0.33); when a spike occurs within the last 3 days of history, the true outcome often reverses direction immediately, whereas the model continues its current trend; apply a directional override rule that flips the forecast slope if sentiment > 0.25 and price change in last 3 days < -5%.",
    "In high-volatility regimes (Example 3-5), the model's linear extrapolation leads to excessive overshoot or undershoot by day 14; reduce the forecast horizon weight for days 8-20 by 40% and increase reliance on recent volatility bands.",
    "When sentiment is consistently zero while price action shows high variance, assume the market is pricing in uncorrelated news events; add a random walk component with standard deviation equal to the historical average daily range to the model output."
  ]
}
